<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
    <PropertyGroup>
        <OutputType>Library</OutputType>
        <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
        <RootNamespace>Bennewitz.Ninja.LayeredEditors.Avalonia.Services</RootNamespace>
        <AssemblyName>LayeredEditors.Avalonia.Services</AssemblyName>
        <Description>
            Platform-agnostic and Avalonia-backed service implementations that are not specific
            to any Claude configuration concern: shell launching, file-manager integration,
            environment-variable access, the Avalonia dialog service (file/folder pickers,
            alerts, confirmations, save-changes prompts), and value converters over the
            product-neutral vocabulary in AgentForge.Abstractions.
        </Description>
        <!-- ⛔ SINGLE TFM, inherited from Directory.Build.props. This project used to declare
             <TargetFrameworks>net10.0;net10.0-windows10.0.19041.0</TargetFrameworks> with a
             comment saying the plural form took precedence over the root's singular
             <TargetFramework>. It does not: MSBuild cross-targets only when TargetFramework is
             EMPTY, and the root sets it. The Windows TFM was therefore never built, in any
             configuration, since it was added — so MAUI Essentials never compiled and
             DefaultShareService has always been its non-Windows fallback.

             Adding a platform TFM back means clearing the root's singular property first, and
             then re-establishing the six-RID zero-warning trim result, because MAUI Essentials
             needed its own ILLink suppression file. See DefaultShareService's remarks. -->
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!-- The solution's friend grants, in one shared file next to the .slnx. LINKED, not
         copied, by a relative path with forward slashes so Linux and macOS resolve it the
         same way Windows does. Read that file before adding a name: it is compiled into
         every assembly, so a grant there applies to all of them. -->
    <ItemGroup>
        <Compile Include="../../AssemblyInfo.InternalsVisibleTo.cs" Link="Properties/AssemblyInfo.InternalsVisibleTo.cs"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <PackageReference Include="Avalonia" Version="12.1.0" />
        <!-- Dialog model types (DialogMessage, DialogCategory, DialogSegment*) live in
             AgentForge.Abstractions. This library previously referenced ClaudeForge.Sdk
             for them, which meant a product-neutral services library depended on a
             Claude-specific SDK purely to name four primitives. Splitting them into the
             abstractions assembly removes the violation with no added indirection —
             this library uses exactly those four types and never SdkDialogs. -->
        <!--
            ⭐ THE EDGE THAT USED TO POINT AT AgentForge, AND NOW DOES NOT.

            This was the ONLY reference from the LayeredEditors family into AgentForge, and it
            was what stopped the editor libraries being liftable into their own repository: a
            generic services package cannot ship without dragging agent-configuration contracts
            with it. Two types crossed here, and they needed opposite fixes:

              • DialogMessage & friends — genuinely generic UI contract, whose own remarks said
                "nothing here knows which product it is serving". MOVED to
                LayeredEditors.Abstractions, where a host-agnostic contract belongs.
              • PermissionOutcome — genuinely agent-domain vocabulary (allow / ask / deny), shared
                because BOTH agent products spell those three answers the same way. It stayed put;
                what moved was PermissionOutcomeToBrushConverter, which had no business being in a
                generic services package and had exactly one consumer, a ClaudeForge view.

            ⚠ The direction of travel is the point: the product repo may depend on the library
            repo, never the reverse.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\LayeredEditors.Abstractions\LayeredEditors.Abstractions.csproj"/>
    </ItemGroup>
</Project>
